# T19-G3

Blocked: T19-G3 requires T19-G1 and T19-G2.  T19-G2 is available, but the
integrated T19-G1 result explicitly records the missing F1 live pair
prerequisite.  A two-identity restart receipt cannot be fabricated without
performing the required two-instance VM run, which this batch forbids.

# T20-C5

Blocked: T20-C5 needs T20-C1's BORROW-CORE.  No
`borrowed_window_lifecycle.rs` implementation is reachable from this branch;
moving WhatsApp QA onto a newly invented private lifecycle would violate the
single-engine requirement the task exists to enforce.

# T20-D1

Blocked: T20-D1 owns the monitor module of T20-C1's absent BORROW-CORE.  The
current browser companion is a separate legacy implementation, so adding a
fourth monitor clamp there would not provide the required all-substrate
guarantee or TW-9 proof.

# T20-E1

Blocked: T20-E1 needs T20-C1 and a live Windows borrowed-window fixture.  No
shared lifecycle or `borrowed_window_death.rs` fixture exists in this branch,
and the required crash/handle-reuse observation cannot be substituted with an
app build or VM run under this batch's constraints.

# T20-E2

Blocked: T20-E2 deliberately extends T20-E1's single TW-13 fixture, which is
absent because E1 is blocked.  A separate process-kill test would split the
required clean-close and crash proof and violate its narrow ownership.

# T20-E5

Blocked: T20-E5 requires the T20-B5 baseline and an interactive Windows VM
with a borrowed surface.  Neither is integrated into this worktree; keyboard
snap measurement must use that real surface and cannot be asserted honestly
from a synthetic script on this Linux host.

# T6-R3

Blocked: T6-R3 depends on T6-R2, but the reachable R2 commit is not integrated
and `prose_token.rs` still uses the explicitly named `legacy_scope_fetch_key`.
Changing only this caller would make sender and receiver capability contracts
diverge, not close the public-scope authority finding.
